# v1.2.1 - 09-01-2026
- Bundle the dependency headers in the release archive
  - `slick/logger.hpp` includes `<slick/queue.h>`, which pulls in `<slick/shm/...>`, so the published archive held a header that could not compile on its own and every consumer had to go and fetch the dependencies separately
  - `slick-logger-<version>.zip`/`.tar.gz` now also carry `slick/queue.h` from slick-queue and the `slick/shm/` tree from slick-shm, with both licences in `licenses/`
  - The release job configures CMake and bundles whatever `slick/` headers the fetched dependencies provide, rather than naming them here. `slick/shm/` comes from slick-shm, a transitive dependency of slick-queue rather than part of it, so an enumerated list would already have been wrong and would break again the next time the graph changes
  - It then compiles `slick/logger.hpp` against the bundled headers alone. A missing header fails the release instead of reaching a user's build
- Clear the wake and flush state in `reset()`
  - `reset()` left `flush_request_`, `flush_done_`, `wake_token_` and `writer_parked_` at whatever the previous run had reached, so a reused `Logger` was not equivalent to a fresh one
  - Purely for clarity, not correctness: the counters are monotonic, the writer compares `flush_request_` against `flush_done_` for inequality rather than order so it resynchronises on its first iteration whatever they hold, and `wake_token_` is only ever compared for change. `reset()` calls `shutdown()` first, so the writer thread is joined before any of this is touched
- Fix a data race on the writer thread's queue cursor
  - `read_index_` was a plain `uint64_t` written by the writer thread and read by `flush()` on the caller's thread. That is a data race under the memory model - undefined behaviour, and reported by ThreadSanitizer - even though it is benign on x86 in practice. Pre-existing, but `flush()` now documents a guarantee built on that read, so the cursor is a `std::atomic<uint64_t>`
  - Relaxed ordering throughout: the cursor carries no data, only progress. What makes `flush()` a real guarantee is the acquire/release `flush_request_`/`flush_done_` handshake, not this value
  - `SlickQueue::read()` is overloaded on `uint64_t&` and `std::atomic<uint64_t>&`, and the atomic overload claims slots with different multi-consumer semantics. The cursor is therefore round-tripped through a local so the single-consumer overload is still the one selected
  - No measurable cost: relaxed loads and stores are ordinary moves on x86. Over interleaved runs of both builds, throughput was 635k vs 650k msg/s and log-call-to-sink latency was mean 25.4us vs 24.2us, p50 22.5us vs 22.4us - unchanged within run-to-run noise
  - Worth keeping: with a plain `uint64_t` the compiler was entitled to hoist the load in `wait_until_ready()` out of its loop and spin on a stale register, so this was not only a sanitizer complaint
- Drop the startup delay in `init()`
  - `init()` slept "to ensure writer thread is started" after launching it. A sleep cannot ensure that, and none was needed: `running_` is already true before the thread is created and the queue is lock-free, so entries logged before the thread is scheduled simply wait in the ring to be drained
  - On Windows the ~15.6ms timer resolution made that nominal 1ms delay the single largest avoidable cost in startup. `init()` p50 65.5ms -> 50.4ms, with the remainder being the queue and string-buffer allocation
- Stop the idle writer thread from sleeping between polls
  - The writer thread called `sleep_for(1ms)` whenever it found no data. Windows' default timer resolution is ~15.6ms, so that request really parked the thread for ~15.6ms; asking for 100us made no difference. Every entry logged into an idle logger waited out that interval before reaching its sink
  - The thread now stays hot for a short burst of `yield()`, which covers the gaps in an active logging stream, and then parks on an atomic until a producer, a flush request, or shutdown releases it. Idle CPU use stays at zero and no timer resolution is involved
  - Producers pay an atomic load and a predictable branch: the wake-up is only signalled when the writer has advertised itself as parked. The load is `seq_cst`, paired with the writer's `seq_cst` store of the same flag - that pairing is what stops the writer's park and a producer's publish from stepping past each other and losing a wake-up on a weakly-ordered CPU. On x86 it still compiles to a plain load
  - `flush()` no longer polls on a coarse sleep either, and both it and `shutdown()` explicitly release a parked writer - `atomic::wait` has no timeout, so a missed wake-up there would block the caller rather than merely delay an entry
  - Parking is limited to `QueueMode::Local`. `writer_parked_` lives in one address space and neither `WaitOnAddress` nor a futex on a private mapping can be signalled from another process, so a collector keeps polling for entries that cross a process boundary
  - Measured from the log call to the sink receiving the entry, on an otherwise idle logger: mean 10986 us -> 25 us, p50 15078 us -> 22 us. `flush()` on an idle logger: p50 16519 us -> 112 us
- Stop flushing sinks after every drained batch
  - The writer thread flushed every sink at the end of each batch. `SlickQueue::read()` hands back one reserved entry at a time, so whenever the writer kept up with its producers that was one `fflush` - and one write syscall - per log entry, costing about 11 us and dwarfing everything else the writer thread did
  - Sinks are now flushed when the queue runs dry and on request, so a burst shares a single flush. What a caller can observe is unchanged: once the logger is caught up, everything logged is on disk
  - `Logger::flush()` previously only waited for the queue cursor to reach the entries it had snapshotted. The cursor advances when the writer *claims* a batch, not when it finishes writing it, so this could return while a sink was still mid-write, and relied on the per-batch flush to get the data out. It now asks the writer thread - the only thread allowed to touch the sinks - for a flush and waits for that specific request to be acknowledged
  - `FileSink` and its subclasses install an explicit 64 KiB stream buffer instead of relying on the default
  - Measured with a file sink: writer-thread cost per entry 13.73 us -> 1.86 us, and end-to-end throughput for 200k messages ~61k -> ~570k msg/s
- Speed up timestamp formatting on the writer thread
  - `TimestampFormatter::format_timestamp` no longer builds every timestamp through `std::ostringstream`/`std::put_time`, which paid for a locale lookup and a stream allocation on every log entry
  - The broken-down time is now converted once per whole second and cached, so a burst of entries inside the same second costs no `localtime` call at all
  - The digits are written directly into a stack buffer with a two-digit lookup table instead of going through `snprintf`, whose format-string parsing dominated what was left
  - The cache is `thread_local` rather than per formatter: its contents do not depend on the output format, so all sinks on the writer thread share one `localtime` call per second, and a `TimestampFormatter` shared between threads stays safe to use
  - Measured on the built-in formats: ~8-10x faster on a realistic log stream, ~3.4x when consecutive entries fall in different seconds. `Format::CUSTOM` still goes through `std::put_time` and is unchanged
  - Output is byte-for-byte identical to the previous implementation, verified across all formats over the epoch, leap days, DST transitions, year boundaries, and every microsecond decade
- Use `std::localtime_s`/`localtime_r` instead of `std::localtime`, which returns a pointer to a shared static buffer and was a data race between threads formatting concurrently
- Stop writing log lines with `std::endl` in the file sinks, which flushed the file stream on every entry; `FileSink`, `RotatingFileSink`, and `DailyFileSink` now write `"\n"` and are flushed when the queue runs dry and on `flush()`. `ConsoleSink` deliberately keeps `std::endl`: `std::cerr` is unit-buffered anyway, an interactive terminal is line-buffered, and when stdout is redirected to a file or pipe a per-line flush is what keeps each line immediately visible to the capturing process (CI logs, `docker logs`, process supervisors) - so the only thing a per-line flush would buy the file sinks is exactly what the batch flush now provides at a fraction of the cost

# v1.2.0 - 08-29-2026
- Add multi-process logging over a shared-memory slick-queue
  - New `LogConfig::mode` selects `QueueMode::Local` (default, unchanged behavior), `QueueMode::SharedProducer`, or `QueueMode::SharedCollector`
  - A collector process owns the sinks and the writer thread; producer processes have neither and only enqueue entries into the shared ring
  - New `LogConfig` fields: `shared_memory_name`, `process_tag`, `collect_backlog`, `stalled_entry_timeout_ms`
  - New `Logger::mode()` and `Logger::shared_memory_name()` accessors
  - Startup order does not matter: whichever process starts first creates the segments and fixes their sizes; later processes attach and inherit them
  - A collector attaching to a segment that already holds entries replays whatever is still resident in the ring (`collect_backlog`, default true)
  - A producer killed between reserve and publish no longer stalls the collector forever; the slot is abandoned after `stalled_entry_timeout_ms` (default 5s) and a warning is logged. The shutdown drain abandons such a slot too, so entries published behind it are not lost
  - No process ever `shm_unlink`s a shared segment on POSIX. Unlinking frees the name while existing mappings stay valid, which would strand attached processes and silently misroute later producers into a fresh segment of the same name. Segments persist in `/dev/shm/` and are removed manually
    - Consequently each `init()`/`shutdown()` cycle that created its segments retains one more mapping for the life of the process. This suits the usual one-shot lifecycle; a process that cycles the logger repeatedly should attach to a segment created elsewhere, which is never retained
  - Entries carry the producing process id and optional tag, rendered as `[pid]` or `[pid:tag]` after the level. Single-process output is unchanged because Local-mode entries carry no pid
    - `process_tag` is capped at 15 bytes and truncated on a UTF-8 character boundary, so a multi-byte tag is never cut mid-sequence
  - When attaching to an existing segment fails and creating one fails too, the reported error carries both reasons, so a creator that was still initializing is not misreported as a size or element-size mismatch
  - Optional `slick_log_collector` executable, built with `-DBUILD_SLICK_LOGGER_COLLECTOR=ON` (OFF by default). Prebuilt Linux, macOS, and Windows binaries are attached to each release
  - New `multi_process_example` demonstrating both roles
- Make log entry string references position independent
  - `StringRef` now holds either a pointer (local mode) or a string-ring index (shared mode), so entries stay meaningful across address spaces
  - In shared mode format strings and source file names are copied into the shared string ring instead of stored as pointers into the producer's read-only data
  - The collector resolves indices back to addresses before dispatching, so sinks always receive resolved pointers and custom sinks need no changes
  - Local mode keeps the zero-copy string-literal fast path; the added producer-side work is a predictable branch on a cached flag
- Fix undefined behavior when formatting log arguments
  - `std::make_format_args` binds a reference to its argument, and `LogArgument` lives in a `#pragma pack(1)` struct, so the reference could bind to a misaligned address (UBSan: "reference binding to misaligned address")
  - Argument values now go through `format_one_arg()`, which takes them by value so the reference binds to an aligned parameter; `view_string_ref()` likewise takes its `StringRef` by value
  - Pre-existing, and reachable from any log call with a multi-byte argument; previously unreported because UBSan only printed rather than failing the build
- Fix the header failing to compile under Clang on x86-64 Linux
  - `enqueue_format_args` tested `std::is_same_v<DT, _Float128>` behind `#ifdef __SIZEOF_FLOAT128__`, but that macro only says the target has a 128-bit float. Clang defines it on x86-64 while providing no `_Float128` type name in C++, and GCC only accepts that spelling in C++ from version 13
  - The ill-formed `if constexpr` condition stopped the chain from discarding later branches, so the `static_cast<double>` was instantiated for every argument type and cascaded into further errors
  - `enqueue_format_args` is a non-template inline function, so it compiles in every translation unit: no Clang-on-Linux build of any file including `slick/logger.hpp` succeeded
  - Replaced with a feature-tested `is_float128_v<T>` trait plus `SLICK_LOGGER_HAS_FLOAT128_T`, keeping the condition well formed everywhere. GCC still matches both `__float128` and `_Float128`; Clang matches `__float128` and never names the unsupported spelling
  - Pre-existing since v1.0.5; CI missed it because Linux used g++, macOS AppleClang on arm64 (where the macro is undefined) and Windows MSVC. A Clang-on-Linux job now covers it
- Fix Release links failing under Clang on Linux
  - `-flto=auto` is a GCC spelling, and Clang's LTO emits LLVM bitcode that GNU ld can only read through a gold plugin built from a matching LLVM version. Distributions routinely ship a mismatched pair, and the link then fails with "LLVM gold plugin has failed to create LTO module: Unknown attribute kind"
  - Clang on Linux now uses ThinLTO through LLD when LLD is present, and skips LTO otherwise. GCC keeps `-flto=auto` and AppleClang keeps LTO through ld64, both unchanged
- Truncate strings longer than 65534 bytes in `store_string_in_queue` instead of overflowing slick-queue's 16-bit reservation size field
- Require slick-queue 1.5.0 or newer, which provides the slick-shm based shared-memory implementation
- CI now builds the collector on every platform, and runs a dedicated ASan/UBSan job with `halt_on_error` so a sanitizer finding fails the build instead of only printing
- Breaking: `LogEntry::format_ptr` and `LogEntry::file_name` are now `StringRef` members named `format` and `file`, with `format_ptr()` and `file_name()` accessors
  - Only affects code that reads or writes those fields directly; sinks built on `format_log_message()` are unaffected
  - Assigning a literal still works via braces, e.g. `entry.format = {"text"};`

# v1.1.4 - 08-15-2026
- Create missing parent directories for file sinks instead of failing to open the log file
  - `FileSink`, `RotatingFileSink`, and `DailyFileSink` now create any missing parent directories before opening their file, matching the "Auto-Creation" behavior the README already documented
  - Previously the `std::ofstream` open failed and the sink constructor threw `Failed to open log file: <path>`, which propagated out of `Logger::init()` / `add_*_file_sink()`
  - Applies to rotation and daily-rollover reopen paths as well, so a directory removed while the logger is running is recreated instead of throwing on the writer thread
  - Directory creation is non-throwing and skipped for paths with no directory component; a genuinely unopenable file still reports the original error
  - Add regression coverage for all three sink types plus `Logger::init(path)` and bare filenames

# v1.1.3 - 08-05-2026
- Fix forwarded `char[]` log arguments being misclassified as string literals
  - Copy `char[N]` and `const char[N]` arguments into the string queue after forwarding so struct member arrays do not leave dangling pointers for the async writer
  - Continue copying `char*`, `const char*`, `std::string`, and `std::string_view` arguments as owned dynamic strings
  - Keep non-string pointer arguments formatted as pointers, including const-qualified pointer types
  - Add regression coverage that mutates a struct member `char[]` after queueing and verifies the original text is logged

# v1.1.2 - 07-30-2026
- Remove full file path logging option
  - `LOG_*` macros and `log_with_location()` now always use the basename; the full-path runtime toggle is gone
  - Remove `LogConfig::include_source_location_full_path`
  - Remove `Logger::set_source_location_full_path_enabled()` and `Logger::source_location_full_path_enabled()`
  - Remove the two-parameter `log_with_location(level, file_path, file_name, line, ...)` overload
  - Remove `StaticSourceLocation::file_path` field and the `SLICK_LOGGER_FILE_PATH` macro
  - Simplify `StaticSourceLocation` constructor and `static_source_location()` to accept only a file name

# v1.1.1 - 07-06-2026
- Use compiler-provided `__FILE_NAME__` for `LOG_*` call-site filenames when predefined
  - Falls back to the internal constexpr basename helper for older GCC/Clang-compatible C++20 compilers and MSVC
  - Keeps `LOG_*` macro source locations in a `static constexpr` call-site path without requiring GCC 12+
- Replace the public `source_location_file_name()` helper with internal `detail::file_name_from_path()` basename handling
  - Preserves dynamic direct-call path support while avoiding an extra public helper API
- Clarify the direct `log_with_location(level, path, line, ...)` overload by treating the argument as a file path
  - Only derives the basename when source-location output is enabled and full-path output is disabled
- Make `log_to_sink_with_location()` public so callers can log to a specific sink while supplying source-location metadata

# v1.1.0 - 07-02-2026
- Add source-location logging for `LOG_*` macros
  - Capture the macro call-site file and line by default
  - Add runtime controls for source-location output and basename vs full-path formatting
  - Add `LogConfig` fields for source-location defaults during logger initialization
  - Add CMake and compile-time controls for disabling source-location capture and overriding the captured file path expression
- Make public direct source-location logging overloads safe for dynamic `const char*` inputs by copying source path data before queueing
- Improve source-location formatting behavior
  - Suppress empty source names instead of emitting empty `[:line]` locations
  - Avoid torn runtime source-location option reads by storing source-location options together
  - Reserve built-in sink output capacity for source-location text to avoid an extra string reallocation
- Fix brace handling in formatted log messages, including escaped braces such as `value={{{}}}` and stray closing-brace errors
- Increase the default string buffer size from 4 MB to 16 MB for queued dynamic string data
- Update the installed CMake package config to require the exported `slick-queue` package and propagate source-location compile definitions through `slick::logger`
- Add regression coverage for source-location defaults, runtime/config controls, dynamic source path copies, empty source suppression, escaped braces, and shared-library plugin call sites
- Update README coverage for source-location logging, runtime controls, timestamp formats, sink lookup, lifecycle APIs, and current example/test targets

# v1.0.9 - 06-11-2026
- Avoid evaluating `LOG_*` arguments when the message level is disabled
  - Move the level gate to the macro boundary so expensive arguments are skipped before `Logger::log(...)` is called
  - Add `Logger::should_log()` as a fast-path runtime check used by the macros
  - Add regression test coverage to verify disabled `LOG_DEBUG` and `LOG_TRACE` calls do not evaluate their arguments

# v1.0.8 - 04-02-2026
- Add cross-DSO logger instance sharing via `Logger::set_instance()` and `Logger::clear_instance_override()`
  - Enables plugin/shared-library architectures where dynamically-loaded modules should route `LOG_*` calls to the host application's logger
  - `instance()` now uses an `std::atomic<Logger*>` initialized to the library-local singleton, swappable at runtime
  - Thread-safe: atomic acquire-release ordering ensures full visibility of the target logger's initialization
- Add `Logger::flush()`: blocks until all queued log entries are written, then returns with the logger still running
  - Use before `FreeLibrary`/`dlclose` to drain entries whose format-string pointers live in the plugin's code segment

# v1.0.7 - 03-30-2026
- Add to_log_level function

# v1.0.6 - 02-13-2026
- Fix logging through downstream `const char*` format bridge with `std::format_args`
  - Dynamic (non-literal) format strings are now copied into the internal string queue so they remain valid across the async writer boundary
  - Dynamic format strings now support arguments instead of requiring zero args
- Fix string queue copy path to null-terminate using explicit payload length
- Add regression test for `const char* + std::format_args` bridge logging

# v1.0.5 - 02-10-2026
- Add `std::format_args` support: pass a pre-built `std::format_args` object directly to any log call
  - Arguments are unpacked and copied into the log entry on the calling thread (safe across thread boundary)
  - Supports all standard `std::basic_format_arg` types: bool, char, int, unsigned, long long, unsigned long long, float, double, long double, const char*, string_view, and void*
  - Custom formatter types (`std::formatter` handle) are not supported; they will be logged as `<handle>`
  - Fix `const void*` handling in `enqueue_argument` (also benefits regular pointer logging)

